ZIF-8 particles adsorption: The adsorption characteristics of progesterone (PGS) by ZIF-8 particles is shown in Fig. 6. The fact that close to $100\%$ removal with almost no detection of the hormone in water after \~ 24 hours confirms the particles hormone capturing stability.
PTFE-ZIF-8 Flow-through Adsorption. Fig. 9 shows the change in PGS mass adsorption with time for batch stirring and batch permeate recycle experiments for the modified membrane. It can be seen that although both approach similar pseudo-steady state conditions at large times, the adsorption rate for the former is higher. The fact that exposure of more membrane interior to PGS under flow through conditions was not as effective as stirring, may suggest limitation by solute transport to the membrane surface, which typically is not a limiting factor in adsorption with stirring.
## 3.3 Progesterone Removal Modeling
The PGS uptake by the membrane may be represented by a simultaneous adsorption and desorption mechanisms [39]. The net rate of PGS disappearance can then be described as:
$$
-\frac{d c\left(t\right)}{d t}=k_{I}c\left(t\right)-k_{2}c_{a}\left(t\right)
$$
$$
\begin{array}{l}{c\left(t\right)=c_{o},t=o,}\\ {c_{a}\left(t\right)=c_{o}-c\left(t\right)}\end{array}
$$
Where $c_{o}$ is the initial PGS concentrations, $c(t)$ its concertation in the solution at time $t$ , $c_{a}(t)$ is the adsorbed PGS concentrations, all in $\mathrm{kg/m}^{3}$ , and the parameters $k_{I}$ , and $k_{2}$ are the forward and backward rate constants, respectively. Solution of Eqs. (1) and (2) can be expressed in terms of  the dimensionless concentration ${\varphi}(t)=c\left(t\right)\big/c_{o}$ as,
$$
\varphi(t)=\frac{1+K_{t}e^{-\left(k_{1}+k_{2}\right)t}}{1+K_{t}}
$$
In which, $K_{t}=k_{I}/k_{2}$ . Eq. (3) was fitted to the experimental date using Levenberg-Marquardt nonlinear fitting algorithm [40, 41]. It can be seen from Table 2 the larger forward adsorption to backward desorption ratio of the modified membrane compared to that without ZIF-8 which reflects the observed higher removal capacity of the former.
Figs. 10a, and 10b show good agreement between the measured and predicted concentrations for the treated and untreated membranes respectively with ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{R}}}^{2}$ value of 0.957, and standard error of 0.047, which supports the modeling approach.
## 3.4 Regeneration
Regeneration of the membrane material was studied using $20~\mathrm{ml}$ of aqueous solutions of two selected solvents, namely, acetone and PEG-400 based on their progesterone solubilises and the presence of a competing hydrogen donor that could destabilize the hydrogen bonds and attract adsorbed PGS into the solvent solution. The regeneration was done three times and the membrane adsorption effectiveness was measured in a similar manner to the batch stirring experiments for fresh membrane using $100\mathrm{ml}$ of $5\mathrm{mg/L}$ initial PGS concentration solutions.
It can be seen from the results in Fig. 11 that although both solvents were capable of facilitating desorption of PGS from the membrane material, PEG-400 was more superior in maintaining close to $95\%$ of the fresh membrane removal efficiency. Based on a wastewater hormone concentration of $\sim100~\mathrm{{ng/L}}$ , which has been typically reported [13-15], the estimated volume reduction ratio would be much higher with less solvent requirement per unit volume treated feed. This significant volume reduction would allow for development of a continuous operation where removal and regeneration are achieved simultaneously with the membrane providing the barrier between the phases thus eliminating the need for an added solvent separation step from the treated water solution. It would also significantly reduce the equipment and processing costs for the solvent regeneration, which can be possibly be done using for example advanced oxidation techniques, thus closes the loop and allows for achieving a near zero emission process.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
A double-layer PTFE membrane material has been modified using ZIF-8 MOFs and tested for decontamination of water from micropollutants using progesterone as a model compound. Incorporation of ZIF-8 provided a synergy of increasing both the membrane adsorption capacity as well as its water permeability. Using a high strength hormone concentrations, the membrane maintained close to $95\%$ of its removal efficiency after three regeneration cycles using PEG-400, which would result in a significant volume reduction ratio. It is proposed that the enhancement in the membrane adsorption can be attributed to the increase in the effective specific area and the formation of hydrogen bond between the hormone (hydrogen acceptor) and ZIF-8 (hydrogen donor). The observed high water flux, which has also been reported by previous investigators, was related to possible improvement to the pore size distribution, which decreases the membrane resistance, as well as the presence of N-H functionality on the ZIF-8 crystal surface which could result in hydrogen bonding with water molecules and
formation of fast water moving channels that increases the membrane water permeance. The advantage provided by the high permeability, which can be orders of magnitudes higher than for example nanofiltration systems, eliminates the need for high pressure pumping which simplifies the process and reduces its specific energy consumption. These, when added to the membrane high adsorption capability, fouling tolerance due to high porosity, relatively low cost, efficient regeneration and ease of operation compared to for example packed bed systems, would significantly reduce both the process capital and operating costs and enhances its overall productivity and cost effectiveness. Such advantages would allow further consideration of the approach for development of cost effective continuous flow membrane based adsorption processes for micropollutants removal from water sources.
